  "nl_text": "The database schema \"rschema_0445\" is a comprehensive database for a security system that manages personnel, access points, and appointments. The schema consists of 13 tables, each representing a different entity or relationship in the system.\nThe \"personnel\" table stores information about the personnel, including their ID, name, type, contact information, health status, entry permission, and the ID of the unit they are affiliated with. The \"unit\" table stores information about the units, including their ID, name, type, and the ID of the administrator in charge.\nThe \"health_record_entry\" table stores information about the health records of the personnel, including the ID of the personnel, the ID of the record, and the time of entry and exit. The \"access_personnel\" table stores information about the access points that personnel have access to, including the ID of the personnel, the ID of the record, and the time of entry and exit.\nThe \"access_point_entry\" table stores information about the access points, including the ID of the access control, the ID of the record, and the time of entry and exit. The \"administrator\" table stores information about the administrators, including their ID, name, and management role.\nThe \"appointment\" table stores information about the appointments, including the ID, scheduled entry and exit times, status, visitor name, and the ID of the personnel being visited. The \"health_record\" table stores information about the health records of the personnel, including the ID of the appointment, scheduled entry and exit times, status, and the ID of the personnel.\nThe \"access_record\" table stores information about the access records, including the ID of the record, entry and exit times, the ID of the personnel, and the ID of the access control. The \"access_control\" table stores information about the access controls, including their ID, name, location, and status.\nThe \"affiliation\" table stores information about the affiliation between personnel and units, including the ID of the personnel and the ID of the unit. The \"management\" table stores information about the management of the units, including the ID of the unit and the ID of the administrator in charge.\nThe \"visit_appointment\" table stores information about the appointments that personnel visit, including the ID of the appointment and the ID of the personnel being visited.\nThe schema is designed to enforce entity integrity, referential integrity, and domain integrity by using primary keys, foreign keys, and constraints. The primary keys ensure that each table has a unique identifier, while the foreign keys establish relationships between tables. The constraints ensure that the data in the tables is consistent and valid.",
  "gold_question": "The campus access control management system functions as follows. For personnel information management, the system maintains basic information for all personnel, with each person having a unique ID, name, personnel type (teacher, student, third-party personnel, visitor), contact phone number, email and other basic information. Each unit has a unique unit ID, unit name, unit type (secondary unit, third-party unit) and other information. Administrators in the system each have a unique administrator ID, name and management role. For health monitoring, the system records health information for each person, including temperature, symptoms, health code color and other data, while tracking personnel travel trajectory information. For visitor management, each visitor appointment record has a unique appointment ID and records visitor ID, visited personnel ID, scheduled entry time, scheduled exit time and appointment status (pending review, approved, rejected) and other information. Visitors initiate appointments by scanning WeChat QR codes, and visited personnel review and confirm through the WeChat platform. For access management, the system records all personnel access records, including access record ID, personnel ID, entry time, exit time and access control device ID information. Each access control device has a unique device ID, device name, device location and device status (normal, fault) information. The system uses dynamic QR codes and facial recognition technology for identity verification, and employs HTTPS encryption for secure data transmission.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_codellama13b/0445.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0445\nCREATE TABLE `personnel` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `contact_info` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `health_status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `entry_permission` boolean NOT NULL,\n  `unit_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `personnel_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`unit_id`) REFERENCES `unit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `unit` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `administrator_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `unit_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`administrator_id`) REFERENCES `administrator` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `health_record_entry` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `record_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `time_entry` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `time_exit` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `health_record_entry_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `health_record_entry_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`record_id`) REFERENCES `health_record` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `access_personnel` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `record_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `time_entry` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `time_exit` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `access_personnel_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `access_personnel_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`record_id`) REFERENCES `access_record` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `access_point_entry` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `access_control_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `record_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `time_entry` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `time_exit` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `access_point_entry_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`access_control_id`) REFERENCES `access_control` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `access_point_entry_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`record_id`) REFERENCES `access_record` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `administrator` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `management_role` varchar(100) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `appointment` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `scheduled_entry` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `scheduled_exit` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `visitor_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `appointment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `health_record` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `appointment_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `scheduled_entry` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `scheduled_exit` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `health_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`appointment_id`) REFERENCES `appointment` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `health_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `access_record` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entry_time` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `exit_time` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `access_control_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `access_record_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `access_record_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`access_control_id`) REFERENCES `access_control` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `access_control` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `location` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `affiliation` (\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `unit_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`personnel_id`, `unit_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `affiliation_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `affiliation_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`unit_id`) REFERENCES `unit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `management` (\n  `unit_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `administrator_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`unit_id`, `administrator_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `management_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`unit_id`) REFERENCES `unit` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `management_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`administrator_id`) REFERENCES `administrator` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `visit_appointment` (\n  `appointment_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `personnel_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`appointment_id`, `personnel_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `visit_appointment_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`appointment_id`) REFERENCES `appointment` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `visit_appointment_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`personnel_id`) REFERENCES `personnel` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
